let's bring in our panel kimberly  strassel member of the editorial board  at the wall street journal steve hayes  editor of the dispatch and harold ford  jr former tennessee congressman ceo of  empowerment and inclusion capital i  would venture a guest kimberly you're  not going to see this topic discussed on  other channels  and other places but your thoughts on  this development what it means big  picture  yeah you already see other channels  dismissing this and suggesting that this  house somehow shows that durham has  coming up short without much much as  they predicted but the reality is this  is kind of an important deal because  let's not forget that the dnc and  hillary clinton used this law firm  to essentially hide  the payments that they were making to  fusion gps in this opposition research  and so what you essentially have is an  indictment of an operative here of the  clinton campaign being indicted for  going to the fbi  uh lying to them in an effort to  manipulate the fbi into investigating a  rival campaign  i think if this was flipped on its head  and this was republicans who did this  it'd be front pages everywhere but it  should be treated as an important moment  yeah steve  where do we think this is going for a  lot of these investigations there is for  people who follow it frustration that it  never ends up in anything  is this something  yeah we need to know more i mean  certainly this is something um it's  never a good idea to lie to the fbi it's  certainly not a good idea to lie to the  fbi in an effort to conceal the fact  that you're working  with a political campaign that may have  been feeding bad information to the u.s  government  we'll see where this goes obviously his  attorneys have put out a statement  saying that this is wrong that he hasn't  committed a crime i'm very interested to  learn to see if we we see from john  durham something that attempts to put  this puzzle piece in a broader picture  so that we could properly understand it  but the story itself is certainly not a  good development for  the justice department for  for michael sussman for the clinton  campaign  harold we're told that durham as the way  he operates  is very deliberate sometimes it takes  time sometimes it takes years uh that's  obviously not satisfying for the for the  trump folks who wanted it to happen  before the election but politically  the upside for the biden folks perhaps  is the merrick garland attorney general  is letting this go forward it doesn't  seem like they're stepping in the way of  any of this  it appears it appears that way thanks  for having me on i don't i don't  disagree with anything that's been said  and don't know much  that i can add other other than to just  echo that this is a serious charge  i've heard those on our network a little  earlier perhaps even a few days ago say  that they didn't think that mr durham  would bring just this charge that he was  probably angling for something else but  this is the charge that he has brought  we'll see the defense from  mr sussman and um we'll let it go from  there but i would imagine to your point  bret that the attorney general is is not  is allowing this to proceed with without  any uh any interference i want to turn  to afghanistan and these questions about  who made the final decision  was president biden kind of out on a  limb on this decision about the exit and  how it happened uh take a listen to the  white house briefing today  did the president  hear specifically the recommendation  from the commander on the ground in  afghanistan that he feared that a full  withdrawal would be  devastating and should not happen he was  provided a range of advice is it's  crystal clear that 2500 troops would not  have been sustainable  on the ground it would have been either  increased troops on the ground or  withdraw troops on the ground he was not  going to send thousands and thousands  more troops to fight a war the afghans  did not want to fight themselves but  kimberly that's the straw man is the  that wasn't the offer and uh from  everything we're getting about general  miller the commander on the ground and  what he recommended and perhaps what  other military advisers were  recommending  uh it was something short of that  yeah i mean the disconnect here all  along is that president biden has been  blaming the military for decisions that  were made there and we're saying hey  this was the best advice and i was just  doing what they told me to do it's on  them if it went wrong but the reality is  and and this you know puts it in better  context is that the president is always  in charge of setting the mission um and  essentially what the military is saying  is that within the parameters he had  given us we didn't have any choice we  had to make that there weren't any  options now we told him you could do it  this way instead and he said no that  won't fit with the mission and so that's  where we got where we got it's on biden  it's it's and he made the call yeah and  obviously that hearing we will get to  september 28th with chairman of the  joint chiefs mark milley uh steve this  will be a major question but as will  obviously the woodward costa book and  all the allegations there  yeah i think we can bet on a number of  questions about the woodward costa book  and the firestorm or the past 36 hours  on this question look  the us military leadership on the ground  in afghanistan  made plenty of mistakes and i think gave  plenty of bad advice over the years they  downplayed the the existence of al qaeda  in country they whitewashed some of the  taliban they did that in public settings  they did that certainly in private  settings but i think kimberly is right  this is ultimately joe biden's call and  there were certainly intelligence  assessments that said to the president  if you withdraw u.s troops if you reduce  the american presence on the ground in  afghanistan it will have negative  effects on what's happening there and  will will hurt our ability certainly to  collect more intelligence so that's on  joe biden

